cells:
  - kind: 1
    value: "# Using Substitution Variables\r

      ## What is a Substitution Variable?\r

      - can be though as a **user variable**\r

      - are placeholders in an SQL script where you want to substitute some
      values at runtime\r

      - ampersand (&) character is used before the substitution variable in the
      query\r

      - If we want to use a substitution variable for a string or a date, we
      need to make sure that the value is enclosed in single quotes\r

      - can used more than one substitution variable at various places in an SQL
      statment like:\r

      \  - where condition\r

      \  - order by clause\r

      \  - column expressions\r

      \  - table name\r

      \  - entire SELECT statements"
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "\r

      select employee_id, first_name, last_name, department_id\r

      from employees\r

      where department_id = 30;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: "\r

      select employee_id, first_name, last_name, department_id\r

      from employees\r

      where department_id = &department_no;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: "\r

      select employee_id, first_name, last_name, department_id\r

      from employees\r

      where first_name = '&name';"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: "\r

      select employee_id, first_name, last_name, &column_name\r

      from &table_name\r

      where &condition\r

      order by &order_by_clause;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "## Double Ampersand (&&) and DEFINE & UNDEFINE Commands\r

      - using a double ampersand in front of a substitution variable tells SQL
      Developer or SQL*Plus to define that variable for the duration of the
      session\r

      \    -  ```sql\r

      \        select select employee_id, first_name, last_name, salary\r

      \        from employees\r

      \        where salary = &&sal\r

      \        or salary = &sal + 1000;\r

      \    ```\r

      - DEFINE and UNDEFINE commands allow us to explicitly create and delete
      user variables\r

      - DEFINE command is used to define a new user-variable and design it a
      value\r

      - DEFINE can also be used to display the value of a specific user variable
      or to display the values of all user variable\r

      \  - ```sql\r

      \    define variable_name = value'\r

      \    ```"
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "-- Display employee information using a user-specified column and sort
      the results \r

      -- by that same column.\r

      select employee_id, first_name, last_name, &&column_name\r

      from employees\r

      order by &column_name;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: "-- Display the unique values of a user-specified column and sort them in
      \r

      -- ascending order.\r

      select &&column_name\r

      from employees\r

      group by &column_name\r

      order by &column_name;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: "-- Display all information for the employee whose employee ID is stored
      in the \r

      -- emp_num substitution variable.\r

      define emp_num = 200;\r

      define column_name = 'first_name';\r

      \r

      select * from EMPLOYEES\r

      where employee_id = &emp_num;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: "-- Remove the emp_num substitution variable and prompt the user to enter
      a new \r

      -- employee ID before executing the query.\r

      define emp_num = 200;\r

      define column_name = 'first_name';\r

      undefine emp_num;\r

      \r

      select * from EMPLOYEES\r

      where employee_id = &emp_num;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: "-- Display all currently defined substitution variables and their
      values.\r

      DEFINE;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: "-- Display the current value of the substitution variable column_name.\r

      DEFINE column_name;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: "-- Remove the column_name substitution variable from the current SQL
      session.\r

      UNDEF column_name;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: "-- Display the current value of the column_name substitution variable.\r

      DEF column_name;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "## ACCEPT and PROMPT Commands\r

      - Most reliable and robust method for getting input from the user is to
      explicitly prompt for values using the ACCEPT and PROMPT commands\r

      \  - ```sql\r

      \     ACCEPT emp_id PROMPT 'Please Enter an Employee ID:';\r

      \    ```\r

      - **ACCEPT** command takes input from the user & stores it in a user
      variable\r

      - **PROMPT** command is used to display a message to the user for
      supplying a brief explanation of what your script is going to accomplish"
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "-- Prompt the user for an employee ID, display the employee's ID, name,
      and \r

      -- salary, then remove the substitution variable.\r

      ACCEPT emp_id PROMPT 'Pleae enter a valid Employee ID:';\r

      SELECT employee_id, first_name, last_name, salary\r

      FROM employees\r

      WHERE employee_id = &emp_id;\r

      \r

      UNDEFINE emp_id;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: "-- Prompt the user for a minimum and maximum salary, display employees
      whose \r

      -- salaries fall within that range, then remove the substitution
      variables.\r

      ACCEPT min_salary PROMPT 'Please specify the MINIMUM salary:';\r

      ACCEPT max_salary PROMPT 'Please specify the MAXIMUM salary:';\r

      SELECT employee_id, last_name, salary\r

      FROM employees\r

      WHERE salary BETWEEN &min_salary AND &maxs_salary;\r

      \r

      UNDEFINE min_salary;\r

      UNDEF max_salary;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "## SET VERIFY ON/SET VERIFY OFF Commands\r

      - **VERIFY** command displays the status of the veriable before and after
      the substitution\r

      - VERIFY command can be used in two ways which are:\r

      \  - ```sql\r

      \     SET VERIFY ON; -- turns on verification\r

      \    ```\r

      \  - ```sql\r

      \     SET VERIFY OFF; -- turn off verification\r

      \    ```"
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "-- Enable substitution variable verification and display the employee's
      ID, \r

      -- first name, and last name for the entered employee ID.\r

      SET VERIFY ON;\r

      SELECT employee_id, first_name, last_name\r

      FROM EMPLOYEES\r

      WHERE employee_id = &emp_id;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: "-- Disable substitution variable verification and display the employee's
      ID, \r

      -- first name, and last name for the entered employee ID.\r

      SET VERIFY OFF;\r

      SELECT employee_id, first_name, last_name\r

      FROM EMPLOYEES\r

      WHERE employee_id = &emp_id;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: "-- Disable verification messages, prompt for an employee ID, display the
      matching \r

      -- employee, then disable substitution variables so 'R&D' is treated as a
      literal \r

      -- string.\r

      SET VERIFY OFF;\r

      SELECT employee_id, first_name, last_name\r

      FROM EMPLOYEES\r

      WHERE employee_id = &emp_id;\r

      \r

      SELECT * FROM departments WHERE department_name = 'R&D';\r

      \r

      SET DEFINE OFF;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
